"ChatGPT Ads expands to Southeast Asia and Taiwan | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/chatgpt-ads-expands-southeast-asia-taiwan/)\nciteturn1171view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1165view0\",\"id\":16}); Total lines: 154\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 23, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Product L16: # ChatGPT Ads expands to Southeast Asia and Taiwan\nL17: \nL18: ChatGPT Ads expands to seven additional markets across Asia and is now available in more than 60 countries.\nL19: \nL20: cite4†Get started with ChatGPT Ads(opens in a new window)†ads.openai.com L21: \nL22: Loading…\nL23: \nL24: Share\nL25: \nL26: ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing\nL27: \nL28:   * cite5†ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing L29:   * cite6†Guided by our ads principles L30:   * cite7†Building a global ads platform L31:   * cite5†ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing L32:   * cite6†Guided by our ads principles L33:   * cite7†Building a global ads platform L34: \nL35: Starting today, ChatGPT Ads will begin rolling out across Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, Singapore, Thailand, Vietnam, and Taiwan. This announcement follows Asia Pacific launches in Australia, New Zealand, Japan, South Korea and India, expanding access for businesses of all sizes across the region.\nL36: Advertisers can access ChatGPT Ads through the OpenAI Ads Solutions team, agency partners including dentsu, Havas Media, Omnicom Media, Publicis Groupe and WPP, and technology partners. Self-service access through cite4†Ads Manager⁠(opens in a new window)†ads.openai.com is also available for eligible businesses.\nL37: Our mission is to ensure that artificial general intelligence benefits all of humanity, and Ads help democratize access to intelligence by supporting free and low-cost access to ChatGPT. As in our existing markets, ads will be shown only to users on the Free and Go plans. Plus, Pro, and Enterprise subscriptions will remain ad-free.\nL38: > “We’re at the beginning of a new chapter for advertising, with AI creating new ways for businesses and people to connect. Our expansion into more markets across Asia brings that opportunity to businesses of every size, from global brands looking for new ways to grow to entrepreneurs and startups running their first campaign.”\nL39: \nL40: —Dave Dugan, Vice President and Head of Global Ads Solutions, OpenAI\nL41: ## ChatGPT Ads: a new channel for marketing\nL42: \nL43: People come to ChatGPT with goals. They might be planning a trip, choosing software for their business, or furnishing a home. Through conversation, people explain what they want to accomplish, what matters to them, and the constraints they face.\nL44: ChatGPT Ads give marketers an opportunity to reach people as they explore and evaluate their options. By introducing relevant products and services, ads can be useful in the moments that spur discovery and shape decisions.\nL45: > “OpenAI is a valued strategic partner for Shopee as we explore how AI can enhance the e-commerce experience for buyers while supporting sellers in growing their businesses. Across our markets, we are expanding access to AI-powered tools that help buyers discover what they need and enable sellers to reach and serve customers more effectively.\nL46: Following the launch of ChatGPT Ads in Brazil, we are pleased to extend this collaboration to Southeast Asia and Taiwan, marking another step in our deepening partnership with OpenAI.”\nL47: —Peggy Zhu, Executive Director, Brand and Growth Marketing, Shopee\nL48: ## Guided by our ads principles\nL49: \nL50: Protecting the trust people place in ChatGPT is foundational to our approach to advertising.\nL51: \nL52: Guided by OpenAI’s cite8†ads principles , we keep conversations private from advertisers and never sell customer data. Ads in ChatGPT are always clearly labeled and separate from ChatGPT’s answers, and advertising does not influence the answers ChatGPT provides. People also have control over ad personalization, with ad-free paid plans available for those who prefer not to see ads.\nL53: Our goal is straightforward: ads should be useful to people as they pursue their goals and make decisions, while also being effective for the businesses that advertise with us.\nL54: ## Building a global ads platform\nL55: At the end of cite9†August , we announced that ChatGPT Ads had reached $1 billion in annualized revenue run rate in less than 200 days after launch. Tens of thousands of advertisers are now running ads in ChatGPT, with many reaching people across multiple countries. This expansion brings that opportunity to more businesses across Southeast Asia and Taiwan. With today’s announcement, ChatGPT Ads are now available in over cite10†60 countries⁠(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com .\nL56: Our progress to date represents only the first chapter in what ChatGPT Ads will become. Over the next few months, we will continue to expand into new markets, build new formats, optimization tools, and measurement solutions. Throughout this work, we remain focused on broadening access to AI, protecting the trust people place in ChatGPT, delivering useful ads to people, and helping businesses achieve their goals.\nL57: Businesses interested in getting started can visit cite4†ads.openai.com⁠(opens in a new window)†ads.openai.com .\nL58:   * cite11†2026 L59:   * cite12†ChatGPT L60: ## Author\nL61: \nL62: OpenAI\nL63: ## Keep reading\nL64: \nL65: cite13†View all L66: \nL67: cite14†Better prompt caching for GPT-6 ProductSep 22, 2026 L68: \nL69: cite15†Introducing GPT-6 Sol and Luna ProductSep 22, 2026 L70: \nL71: cite16†Reimagining advertising with AI ProductSep 16, 2026 L72: \nL73: Research\nL74: \nL75:   * cite17†Research Index L76:   * cite18†Research Overview L77:   * cite19†Economic Research L78: \nL79: Latest Advancements\nL80: \nL81:   * cite20†GPT-6 L82:   * cite21†GPT-5.6 L83:   * cite22†GPT-5.5 L84:   * cite23†GPT-5.4 L85: \nL86: Safety\nL87:   * cite24†Safety Approach L88:   * cite25†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L89:   * cite26†Security & Privacy L90:   * cite27†Trust & Transparency L91: \nL92: Products\nL93: \nL94:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L95:   * cite28†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L96:   * cite29†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L97:   * cite30†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L98:   * cite31†Codex L99:   * cite32†Release Notes L100: \nL101: API Platform\nL102:   * cite33†Overview L103:   * cite34†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L104:   * cite35†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L105: \nL106: Business\nL107: \nL108:   * cite36†Overview L109:   * cite37†Solutions L110:   * cite38†Resources L111:   * cite39†Plugins L112:   * cite40†Customer Stories L113:   * cite41†Partner Network L114:   * cite42†Contact Sales L115: \nL116: Developers\nL117:   * cite43†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L118:   * cite44†Open Models L119:   * cite45†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L120:   * cite46†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L121:   * cite47†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L122: \nL123: Company\nL124: \nL125:   * cite48†About Us L126:   * cite49†Our Charter L127:   * cite50†Careers L128:   * cite13†News L129: \nL130: Support\nL131: \nL132:   * cite51†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L133: \nL134: More\nL135:   * cite52†Stories L136:   * cite53†Academy L137:   * cite54†Supply Co. L138:   * cite55†Livestreams L139:   * cite56†Podcast L140:   * cite57†RSS L141: \nL142: Terms & Policies\nL143: \nL144:   * cite58†Terms of Use L145:   * cite59†Privacy Policy L146:   * cite60†Other Policies L147: \nL148: cite61†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite62†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite63†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite64†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite65†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite66†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite67†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L149: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL150: \nL151: English United States\nL152: \nL153: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAirbnb widens access to GPT-6 Astra and OpenAI frontier models | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/airbnb-gpt-6-astra/)\nciteturn1171view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1165view0\",\"id\":17}); Total lines: 142\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 23, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Company L16: # Airbnb widens access to GPT‑6 Astra and OpenAI frontier models\nL17: \nL18: Loading…\nL19: \nL20: Share\nL21: \nL22: Under a new agreement, Airbnb is giving its engineering and product development teams broader access to OpenAI frontier models, including GPT‑6 Astra.\nL23: \nL24: The expansion builds on Airbnb’s use of Codex and longstanding collaboration with OpenAI. Airbnb engineers use an internal AI assistant for writing software and creating remote AI agents powered by Codex and models like GPT‑5.6 Sol, Terra, and Luna.\nL25: The new agreement gives Airbnb broader access to OpenAI models through OpenAI APIs and Amazon Bedrock. It arrives amid accelerating adoption of GPT‑6 Astra among developers, builders, and enterprises, many of which were already benefiting from the improved cite4†price-performance and efficiency of GPT‑5.6 Terra and Luna.\nL26: Airbnb’s early use of GPT‑6 Astra has shown strong results beyond coding. Astra helps Airbnb engineers accelerate their work by tracking down hard bugs, shaping system designs, and brainstorming engineering approaches. In one test involving strategic documents and other non-coding work, one Airbnb user reported reaching impressive output in 3–4 passes, compared with 20+ rounds using other models.\nL27: > “Our development teams are shipping roughly 80% more features than a year ago. OpenAI’s frontier models, including GPT‑6 Astra, are a key element of our developer tooling that helps us keep that momentum going and build better products faster.”\nL28: \nL29: —Ahmad Al-Dahle, Chief Technology Officer at Airbnb\nL30: > “Airbnb has been using AI to rethink how software gets built, and expanding access to models like GPT‑6 Astra gives its teams even more powerful tools to move from ideas to production faster. We’re proud to deepen our work together as Airbnb puts OpenAI capabilities to work across engineering and the experiences it builds for millions of guests and hosts.”\nL31: \nL32: —Dali Rajic, Chief Revenue Officer at OpenAI\nL33: \nL34: 1 of 2\nL35: > “Our development teams are shipping roughly 80% more features than a year ago. OpenAI’s frontier models, including GPT‑6 Astra, are a key element of our developer tooling that helps us keep that momentum going and build better products faster.”\nL36: \nL37: —Ahmad Al-Dahle, Chief Technology Officer at Airbnb\nL38: > “Airbnb has been using AI to rethink how software gets built, and expanding access to models like GPT‑6 Astra gives its teams even more powerful tools to move from ideas to production faster. We’re proud to deepen our work together as Airbnb puts OpenAI capabilities to work across engineering and the experiences it builds for millions of guests and hosts.”\nL39: \nL40: —Dali Rajic, Chief Revenue Officer at OpenAI\nL41: \nL42:   * Ahmad Al-Dahle\nL43:   * Dali Rajic\nL44: Airbnb also uses OpenAI models to help support experiences across search, fraud prevention, guest and host support, and insurance claims. This deep use of AI and machine learning across its marketplace helps the company better understand what guests are looking for, identify potentially fraudulent content or claims, help guests when travel plans change, and ensure hosts get insurance payouts faster and easier.\nL45: As Airbnb brings more of the trip into one app—across homes, services, experiences, airport pickups, car rentals, and more—OpenAI frontier models can give the teams building those features more powerful tools to move faster and explore what comes next.\nL46: \nL47:   * cite5†2026 L48: ## Author\nL49: \nL50: OpenAI\nL51: ## Keep reading\nL52: \nL53: cite6†View all L54: \nL55: cite7†Two years of OpenAI Academy CompanySep 23, 2026 L56: \nL57: cite8†Advisory group on mathematics and artificial intelligence CompanySep 21, 2026 L58: \nL59: cite9†Expanding OpenAI Academy with new learning paths CompanySep 21, 2026 L60: \nL61: Research\nL62: \nL63:   * cite10†Research Index L64:   * cite11†Research Overview L65:   * cite12†Economic Research L66: \nL67: Latest Advancements\nL68: \nL69:   * cite13†GPT-6 L70:   * cite14†GPT-5.6 L71:   * cite15†GPT-5.5 L72:   * cite16†GPT-5.4 L73: \nL74: Safety\nL75:   * cite17†Safety Approach L76:   * cite18†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L77:   * cite19†Security & Privacy L78:   * cite20†Trust & Transparency L79: \nL80: Products\nL81: \nL82:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L83:   * cite21†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L84:   * cite22†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L85:   * cite23†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L86:   * cite24†Codex L87:   * cite25†Release Notes L88: \nL89: API Platform\nL90:   * cite26†Overview L91:   * cite27†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L92:   * cite28†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L93: \nL94: Business\nL95: \nL96:   * cite29†Overview L97:   * cite30†Solutions L98:   * cite31†Resources L99:   * cite32†Plugins L100:   * cite33†Customer Stories L101:   * cite34†Partner Network L102:   * cite35†Contact Sales L103: \nL104: Developers\nL105:   * cite36†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L106:   * cite37†Open Models L107:   * cite38†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L108:   * cite39†Resources(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L109:   * cite40†Developer Forum(opens in a new window)†community.openai.com L110: \nL111: Company\nL112: \nL113:   * cite41†About Us L114:   * cite42†Our Charter L115:   * cite43†Careers L116:   * cite6†News L117: \nL118: Support\nL119: \nL120:   * cite44†Help Center(opens in a new window)†help.openai.com L121: \nL122: More\nL123:   * cite45†Stories L124:   * cite46†Academy L125:   * cite47†Supply Co. L126:   * cite48†Livestreams L127:   * cite49†Podcast L128:   * cite50†RSS L129: \nL130: Terms & Policies\nL131: \nL132:   * cite51†Terms of Use L133:   * cite52†Privacy Policy L134:   * cite53†Other Policies L135: \nL136: cite54†(opens in a new window)†x.com cite55†(opens in a new window)†www.youtube.com cite56†(opens in a new window)†www.linkedin.com cite57†(opens in a new window)†github.com cite58†(opens in a new window)†www.instagram.com cite59†(opens in a new window)†www.tiktok.com cite60†(opens in a new window)†discord.gg L137: OpenAI © 2015–2026[Button: Manage Cookies]\nL138: \nL139: English United States\nL140: \nL141: [Input: Search]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBetter prompt caching for GPT-6 | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/better-prompt-caching-for-gpt-6/)\nciteturn1171view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1165view0\",\"id\":18}); Total lines: 179\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * [Button: Research]\nL3:   * [Button: Products]\nL4:   * [Button: Business]\nL5:   * [Button: Developers]\nL6:   * [Button: Company]\nL7:   * cite1†Foundation(opens in a new window)†openaifoundation.org L8: \nL9: cite2†Try ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com [Button: Login]\nL10: \nL11: OpenAI\nL12: \nL13: September 22, 2026\nL14: \nL15: cite3†Product L16: # Better prompt caching for GPT‑6\nL17: \nL18: Higher cache hit rates and new tools to help persistent agents run faster and cost less.\nL19: \nL20: Loading…\nL21: \nL22: Share\nL23: \nL24: Monitor caching and diagnose cache misses\nL25: \nL26:   * cite4†Monitor caching and diagnose cache misses L27:   * cite5†Optimize caching for your application L28:   * cite6†Get started L29: \nL30:   * cite4†Monitor caching and diagnose cache misses L31:   * cite5†Optimize caching for your application L32:   * cite6†Get started L33: GPT‑6 enables persistent agents to work for hours on complex tasks, from refactoring codebases to producing well-researched documents and presentations. The applications behind these agents make a series of API requests that build on one another, often carrying forward the same instructions, tool definitions, and context from earlier turns. OpenAI caches that shared context to reuse computation across requests, reducing response times and giving developers discounts of up to 90% on cached input tokens.\nL34: With the GPT‑6 family, we launched an improved prompt caching system that delivers higher cache hit rates by default. We now give cache discounts for eligible shared prefixes reused within a 30-minute window. We’re also introducing new tools to help developers monitor cache performance, diagnose misses, and choose how much of a prompt to cache.\nL35: > “OpenAI’s prompt caching plays a critical role in helping GitHub Copilot deliver fast, efficient experiences at scale. Over the past several months, we’ve reduced by more than 50% the share of prompt tokens requiring fresh processing across billions of requests to OpenAI models, relative to our previous baseline. The result is a more efficient inference stack and faster time to first response for developers.”\nL36: \nL37: —Mario Rodriguez, Chief Product Officer\nL38: ## Monitor caching and diagnose cache misses\nL39: \nL40: The new cite7†Prompt Caching Dashboard⁠(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com shows how much of your application’s input is served from cache. Track hit rates over time and use the input composition chart to compare cached and uncached tokens. These views help you spot drops in cache hits and evaluate how changes to your application impact caching performance.\nL41: When you see an unexpected cache miss, use the cite8†prompt caching diagnostics tool⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com to understand what happened. Compare a request with a recent response to identify changes to the model, tools, settings, or input that prevented reuse. The estimated number of affected tokens helps you assess the size of the impact and decide how you can optimize your integration to maximize cache hit rates.\nL42: \nL43:     `{\nL44:       \"prompt_cache_diagnostics\": {\nL45:         \"type\": \"cache_miss\",\nL46:         \"reason\": \"tools_changed\",\nL47:         \"comparison_reusable_tokens\": 5629,\nL48:         \"cache_missed_tokens\": 5629\nL49:       }\nL50:     }\nL51:     `\nL52: ## Optimize caching for your application\nL53: \nL54: Choose what to cache. Explicit cache breakpoints let you choose which prompt prefixes to reuse. The refreshed cite9†prompt caching guide⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com explains how to use them, how long cached prefixes remain eligible, and how changes to tools and inputs affect reuse.\nL55: Adjust reasoning effort without breaking cache. On GPT‑6 models, you can now cite10†change reasoning effort⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com between responses without breaking cache. Raise effort for a harder task or lower it for a routine follow-up by appending a `configuration_update` while leaving request-level reasoning effort unchanged. This lets you adjust how much reasoning a task needs while preserving reusable context.\nL56: Preserve cache as tools and instructions change. As your agent’s tool use needs change, keep tool definitions, schemas, and ordering stable so earlier context stays reusable. Use `allowed_tools` to make only the relevant tools callable, or set tool_choice to none when no tools are needed, instead of removing definitions. Use new developer messages to append new instructions towards the end of the context to override older ones.\nL57: See our cite11†guidance on managing tool changes⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com .\nL58: Prewarm the cache to reduce latency. cite12†Prewarming⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com prepares known context ahead of time so the model can start responding sooner when a request arrives. For example, an application can prewarm shared instructions, tool definitions, or reference material during startup, before the user asks their first question. This moves processing out of the user’s wait time.\nL59: These optional controls build on the engine’s default performance, helping you tailor caching to your workload.\nL60: \nL61: 1 of 3\nL62: > “OpenAI’s prompt caching diagnostics and dashboard helped us improve cache hit rates by a few percentage points, reducing costs by 20%. We now get alerts when caching breaks unexpectedly and use Codex agents to diagnose the root cause. Explicit breakpoints also let us cache stable context while keeping frequently changing content at the end of the prompt. That’s made it economically viable to fork conversations for background tasks while reusing nearly all of the shared context.”\nL63: —Arian Hanifi, Chief Technology Officer\nL64: > “For long-running agents like Manus, reliable caching is fundamental to the economics. Working with OpenAI's engineering team, we refined cache breakpoint placement, combined explicit and automatic caching, and used real requests to pinpoint unexpected cache misses. In less than a week, our OpenAI model cache hit rate went from roughly 85% to consistently above 90%, further lowering inference costs in production.\nL65: The progress came through a series of targeted improvements, with both teams validating the results along the way.”\nL66: —Bin Fan, Agent Team Lead\nL67: \nL68: > “Working with OpenAI, we moved our session agents to explicit cache breakpoints. In under a week, cache hit rates on our evaluations rose from 83% to 91%. This meant fewer cache writes and lower inference costs with the same workload; the cache writes fell by roughly two-thirds, and inference costs by 36%.”\nL69: \nL70: —Eugene Mikhantyev, AI Engineer\nL71: \nL72:   * Strawberry Browser\nL73:   * Manus\nL74:   * Wordsmith\nL75: ## Get started\nL76: \nL77:   * Monitor cache hit rates in the cite7†Prompt Caching Dashboard⁠(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com .\nL78: \nL79:   * Investigate unexpected misses with the cite8†diagnostics tool⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com .\nL80: \nL81:   * Follow the cite9†prompt caching guide⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com to improve your setup, or cite13†use Codex⁠(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com to review your code, apply improvements, and measure results.\nL82: \nL83:   * cite14†API L84:   * cite15†2026 L85: ## Author\nL86: \nL87: OpenAI\nL88: ## Keep reading\nL89: \nL90: cite16†View all L91: \nL92: cite17†ChatGPT Ads expands to Southeast Asia and Taiwan ProductSep 23, 2026 L93: \nL94: cite18†Introducing GPT-6 Sol and Luna ProductSep 22, 2026 L95: \nL96: cite19†Reimagining advertising with AI ProductSep 16, 2026 L97: \nL98: Research\nL99: \nL100:   * cite20†Research Index L101:   * cite21†Research Overview L102:   * cite22†Economic Research L103: \nL104: Latest Advancements\nL105: \nL106:   * cite23†GPT-6 L107:   * cite24†GPT-5.6 L108:   * cite25†GPT-5.5 L109:   * cite26†GPT-5.4 L110: \nL111: Safety\nL112:   * cite27†Safety Approach L113:   * cite28†Deployment Safety(opens in a new window)†deploymentsafety.openai.com L114:   * cite29†Security & Privacy L115:   * cite30†Trust & Transparency L116: \nL117: Products\nL118: \nL119:   * cite2†ChatGPT(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L120:   * cite31†ChatGPT Business(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L121:   * cite32†ChatGPT Enterprise(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L122:   * cite33†ChatGPT for Education(opens in a new window)†chatgpt.com L123:   * cite34†Codex L124:   * cite35†Release Notes L125: \nL126: API Platform\nL127:   * cite36†Overview L128:   * cite37†API Log In(opens in a new window)†platform.openai.com L129:   * cite38†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L130: \nL131: Business\nL132: \nL133:   * cite39†Overview L134:   * cite40†Solutions L135:   * cite41†Resources L136:   * cite42†Plugins L137:   * cite43†Customer Stories L138:   * cite44†Partner Network L139:   * cite45†Contact Sales L140: \nL141: Developers\nL142:   * cite46†Apps SDK(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L143:   * cite47†Open Models L144:   * cite48†Docs(opens in a new window)†developers.openai.com L145:   * cite49†Resour